{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"d0ed3da935143671","name":"card_event_lure","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Britain/CovertOp/events/card_event_lure","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"LURE","text":"When the enemy gives the second order this turn, counter it.","flavorText":"During WWII the British military employed paradummies, also known as lures, as part of their deceptive tactics. These dummy paratroopers were made from burlap and filled with straw or green waste, and designed to resemble real soldiers when dropped from aircraft. The purpose of these paradummies was to lure enemy forces into believing that a larger airborne assault was underway, thereby diverting their attention and resources from the actual objectives. These decoys were often equipped with simple parachutes to simulate a real drop and were strategically dropped to confuse enemy reconnaissance and intelligence efforts. The paradummies were used during the Normandy landings in 1944, and some were also fitted with a timed detonator to ensure they exploded on landing, destroying evidence of the trick. This gave the illusion that the supposed paratrooper had burned his parachute upon landing and was now avoiding detection.","faction":"Britain","type":"gotcha","rarity":"Common","cost":2,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_lure","title":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_lure_title","SourceString":"LURE","LocalizedString":"LURE"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::gotcha","faction":"EFactionEnum::Britain","kredits":2,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_lure_text","SourceString":"When the enemy gives the second order this turn, counter it.","LocalizedString":"When the enemy gives the second order this turn, counter it."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_lure_history","SourceString":"During WWII the British military employed paradummies, also known as lures, as part of their deceptive tactics. These dummy paratroopers were made from burlap and filled with straw or green waste, and designed to resemble real soldiers when dropped from aircraft. The purpose of these paradummies was to lure enemy forces into believing that a larger airborne assault was underway, thereby diverting their attention and resources from the actual objectives. These decoys were often equipped with simple parachutes to simulate a real drop and were strategically dropped to confuse enemy reconnaissance and intelligence efforts. The paradummies were used during the Normandy landings in 1944, and some were also fitted with a timed detonator to ensure they exploded on landing, destroying evidence of the trick. This gave the illusion that the supposed paratrooper had burned his parachute upon landing and was now avoiding detection.","LocalizedString":"During WWII the British military employed paradummies, also known as lures, as part of their deceptive tactics. These dummy paratroopers were made from burlap and filled with straw or green waste, and designed to resemble real soldiers when dropped from aircraft. The purpose of these paradummies was to lure enemy forces into believing that a larger airborne assault was underway, thereby diverting their attention and resources from the actual objectives. These decoys were often equipped with simple parachutes to simulate a real drop and were strategically dropped to confuse enemy reconnaissance and intelligence efforts. The paradummies were used during the Normandy landings in 1944, and some were also fitted with a timed detonator to ensure they exploded on landing, destroying evidence of the trick. This gave the illusion that the supposed paratrooper had burned his parachute upon landing and was now avoiding detection."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Common","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::CurrentExpansion","isReserved":true,"usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardPlayedFromHand"],"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_U